---
/**
 * /getting-paid/ — what it costs a small business to be paid, and by whom.
 *
 * WHY IT IS TOP LEVEL RATHER THAN UNDER /websites/. The website comparison sits
 * at /websites/compare/ because /websites/ sells the thing it compares against.
 * Nothing on this page is for sale. A business with no website at all still has
 * to be paid, and burying this under the website product would have scoped it to
 * people already buying one.
 *
 * THE AUDIENCE IS WIDER THAN THE WEBSITE SHEET'S, DELIBERATELY. Any small
 * business, including one that only exists online and sells across a border —
 * D29 says local is the home base and not a fence. That is what put the
 * merchant-of-record card on the sheet and what made two worked examples
 * necessary instead of one, because three of the nine options refuse to price
 * one of the two and a single example would have hidden that.
 *
 * NOTHING HERE IS SOLD AND THE PAGE HAS TO SAY SO EARLY. The offer card exists
 * so the sheet is honest about who wrote it, not so it can be pitched: the
 * account opens in the reader's name, we take no cut, and connecting a payment
 * link to something we build is separate work quoted separately. Section 3 of
 * both website Scopes excludes taking payments and this page does not walk that
 * back by a single word.
 *
 * EVERY OPTION MUST KEEP ITS `doesBetter` ROW AND A GATE ENFORCES IT.
 * scripts/verify-getpaid.mjs fails if any option's text is missing from the
 * rendered page and refuses one under 100 characters. Nobody deletes that row on
 * purpose; it gets softened in a copy pass until it says nothing, and a
 * comparison with no losing rows is an advertisement wearing a table.
 *
 * NO FIGURE IS TYPED IN THIS FILE. Every rate comes out of the data module and
 * every total is computed by src/lib/payment-cost.js, so a Node gate checks the
 * same arithmetic the page runs rather than a copy of it.
 */
import MarketingLayout from '../layouts/MarketingLayout.astro';
import WhatIBuild from '../components/WhatIBuild.astro';
import { options, rows, READ_ON, EXAMPLES } from '../data/getting-paid.js';
import { compareOptions, annualSpread, usd, pct } from '../lib/payment-cost.js';
import { ArrowUpRight } from '@lucide/astro';

const origin = new URL(Astro.site!).origin;

/* Computed once, here, so no arithmetic happens inside a template. */
const tables = EXAMPLES.map((example) => {
  const table = compareOptions(options, example);
  return { example, table, spread: annualSpread(table) };
});

const counter = tables[0];
const online = tables[1];

const schema = [
  {
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: `${origin}/` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Getting paid', item: `${origin}/getting-paid/` },
    ],
  },
];
---
<MarketingLayout
  schema={schema}
  title="What it costs your business to be paid"
  description="Stripe, Square, PayPal, Venmo, Clover, Toast and the rest, read off their own pricing pages, with the arithmetic on your own numbers. Nothing here is for sale."
  image="/og/websites.png"
>
  {/* ── Hero ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden border-b border-line">
    <div class="bg-grid bg-grid-fade pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 -z-10 opacity-60"></div>
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 pt-16 pb-10 sm:pt-24 sm:pb-14">
      <p class="mono-label">Every option, read off their own pricing pages on {READ_ON}</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 max-w-4xl font-display text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-5xl">
        You know your headline rate. <span class="aiq-accent">You almost certainly do not know what you pay.</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        A rate is not a price. 2.9% plus 30 cents and 2.6% plus 15 cents cannot be ranked without
        knowing the size of your average sale, because on a small one the fixed part is most of the
        cost and on a large one it rounds to nothing. So this page does the sum instead of ranking
        them.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        I sell none of these and I take no cut of anything you sell. That is the only reason this
        page is worth reading rather than the {options.length} others you will find, which were mostly
        written by one of the companies on it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── The arithmetic, first, because the answer is often "change nothing" ─ */}
  <section class="section-band border-b border-line">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 py-14 sm:py-20">
      <p class="mono-label">The arithmetic</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 max-w-3xl font-display text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">
        Three numbers of yours, and every option below has a real price.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        What goes through the card in a month, what an average sale is, and how much of it happens in
        person. That is all it takes. Two worked examples follow so you can see the shape — put your
        own three numbers in their place and the sum is identical.
      </p>

      {tables.map(({ example, table, spread }) => (
        <div class="mt-10">
          <p class="mono-label">{example.title}</p>
          <p class="mt-2 max-w-2xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-faint">
            Assumed, not measured: {example.label}. These are my numbers for the example, not a claim
            about what your business does.
          </p>

          {/* The table is the one element allowed past the body width, and it
              scrolls inside its own box so the page itself never does.
              verify-mobile.mjs measures element boxes against the viewport. */}
          <div class="mt-5 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-line">
            <table class="w-full min-w-[36rem] border-collapse text-left text-sm">
              <thead>
                <tr class="border-b border-line">
                  <th class="p-4 font-display font-semibold text-ink">Option</th>
                  <th class="p-4 font-display font-semibold text-ink">A month</th>
                  <th class="p-4 font-display font-semibold text-ink">A year</th>
                  <th class="p-4 font-display font-semibold text-ink">What that really is, as a rate</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {table.map((r) => (
                  <tr class={`border-b border-line/60 ${r.id === 'aiq' ? 'bg-accent/5' : ''}`}>
                    <td class="p-4 align-top">
                      <span class="font-semibold text-ink">{r.name}</span>
                    </td>
                    {r.cents === null ? (
                      <td class="p-4 align-top text-ink-faint" colspan="3">{r.problem}</td>
                    ) : (
                      <>
                        <td class="p-4 align-top text-ink-muted">{usd(r.cents)}</td>
                        <td class="p-4 align-top text-ink-muted">{usd(r.annualCents)}</td>
                        <td class="p-4 align-top text-ink-muted">{pct(r.effectiveHundredths)}</td>
                      </>
                    )}
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-faint">
            Cheapest to dearest, with the ones that cannot be worked out kept at the bottom rather
            than dropped. The gap between the top and the bottom of what could be priced is
            {' '}{usd(spread.differenceCents)} a year on those numbers.
          </p>
        </div>
      ))}

      <div class="panel mt-10 max-w-3xl p-7">
        <p class="font-display text-xl font-semibold text-ink">
          Usually the answer is to change nothing, and know your number.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
          Moving processor costs you a weekend and loses your saved cards, so unless the gap is four
          figures a year it is not worth doing. The reason to read this is so that nobody can quote
          you a headline again without you knowing what it turns into.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-faint">
        What the sum assumes, stated plainly: today's advertised rates, every sale the same size, and
        no card crossing a border. That last one is left out on purpose — currency conversion is
        charged on top and the spread runs from one percent to four across this page, so it cannot be
        worked out without knowing which currencies you take. It is arithmetic on published figures
        rather than a forecast, and the moment it needs a forecast it stops belonging here.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── The cards. Same rows, same order, so a reader compares down. ──────── */}
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-14 sm:py-20">
    <p class="mono-label">One at a time</p>
    <h2 class="mt-3 max-w-3xl font-display text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">
      The same {rows.length} questions, asked of every one of them.
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
      Grouped by how you ended up with the rate you have, because for most businesses nobody ever
      chose it. Mine is on the list and gets the same treatment as the rest.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
      {options.map((o) => (
        <div class={`panel lift flex flex-col gap-4 p-7 ${o.isMine ? 'border-accent/40' : ''}`}>
          <div>
            <p class={`mono-label ${o.isMine ? 'text-cyan-deep' : ''}`}>{o.kind}</p>
            <h3 class="mt-2 font-display text-xl font-semibold leading-snug text-ink">{o.name}</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-ink-faint">{o.how}</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-ink-muted">{o.headline}</p>
          </div>
          <dl class="flex flex-col gap-3 border-t border-line pt-4">
            {rows.map((r) => (
              <div>
                <dt class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-ink-faint">
                  {r.label}
                </dt>
                <dd class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">{o[r.key]}</dd>
              </div>
            ))}
          </dl>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── The one that earns the page ───────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="section-band border-y border-line">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-14 sm:py-20">
      <p class="mono-label">Why check any of this yourself</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
        The comparison you find is usually written by somebody on it.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        Intuit publishes a table comparing QuickBooks against Stripe and Square. It is stamped
        accurate as of the thirtieth of April, and on the day I read all three pages together it
        showed Stripe's bank transfer rate at 1.2% where Stripe's own page says 0.8% capped at five
        dollars, and Square in person at 2.6% plus 10 cents where Square's own page says 15.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        That is staleness rather than dishonesty — they dated it and they said it shows only the
        nearest plan. But it is four months old on a page selling you something, and it is the reason
        every figure here carries the date I read it and a link you can check me against.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── The same three lists that end every sheet here ─────────────────────
      SHARED WITH /websites/compare/ AND /stores/, out of src/lib/capabilities.js.
      The offer card two sections up says what I do; this says what I will not,
      and the second is the half a reader cannot get from anybody selling. */}
  <WhatIBuild sheet="payments" />

  {/* ── What this is not, matching the /websites/compare/ and /check/ pattern */}
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-14 sm:py-20">
    <p class="mono-label">What this is not</p>
    <h2 class="mt-3 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
      Not a recommendation, and not something I sell.
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
      I am not a payment processor, I take no percentage of anything you sell, and I earn nothing
      whichever of these you pick. The account opens in your name and stays yours, always. If I am
      building you a site, one payment link connected to one page comes with it; opening a processor
      account from scratch is quoted first, because that runs on their identity checks rather than on
      my time. A store with a basket and stock counts is separate work with its own price, and I do
      not build shipping by address or sales tax by state at any price.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
      It is also not tax advice, and the line matters. The cards say who the merchant of record is,
      because that decides who owes the sales tax. What they will never do is work out whether you
      owe any. That is a question for your accountant and the answer is worth paying for.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
      And it is not a list I keep current by remembering to. Every figure above is registered with
      the date it was read, it expires on its own, and this page carries that date in plain sight.
      One line on this site went stale exactly that way and the register is what caught it.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-10 border-t border-line pt-10">
      <p class="mono-label">If you want the sum done on your own numbers</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
        Send me the three numbers and I will send the table back.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        A month of card volume, an average sale, and roughly how much of it is taken in person. No
        charge and no meeting — you will get the same table you see above with your figures in it,
        and often the answer is that you are already in the right row.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
        <a href="/contact/?intent=question" class="btn btn-primary">
          Send me the three numbers <ArrowUpRight class="h-4 w-4" aria-hidden="true" />
        </a>
        <a href="/websites/compare/" class="btn btn-secondary">The same reading, for websites</a>
        {/* THE CROSS-REFERENCE THE MERCHANT-OF-RECORD CARD ASKS FOR. That card
            says somebody else becoming the legal seller is what moves the tax
            liability. A marketplace does the same thing to a physical seller
            without ever using the phrase, and the store sheet is where that is
            answered platform by platform. Prose cannot carry a link, so the
            card names the other sheet and this is where a reader can reach
            it. */}
        <a href="/stores/" class="btn btn-secondary">And for selling online</a>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-6 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-faint">
        Every rate and every term on this page was read off those companies' own pages on
        {' '}{READ_ON}. They change them without announcing it, so if you are weighing this up, go
        and read them again yourself.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</MarketingLayout>
